  "slug": "maternal-remodeling-efae",
  "summary": "New preprints use scRNAseq and spatial transcriptomics to map state-dependent remodeling in liver, lung, adipose, mammary, cervical and gut tissue.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is single-cell and spatial transcriptomic mapping of how tissues reorganize across physiological, pathological and regenerative states. In human metabolic liver disease, senescent SenSpots localize to portal tracts and fibrotic septa, with phenotypes shifting during progression. Aging male mouse inguinal white adipose tissue contains lipid-associated macrophages with age-linked inflammatory and metabolic features. A rat post-pneumonectomy atlas combines scRNAseq, Xenium and NICHES connectomics across post-operative days to resolve compensatory lung regrowth. Saanen goat mammary tissue shows period-specific epithelial, stromal and immune remodeling between dry period and peak lactation. Two studies address baseline or environmental context rather than remodeling trajectories: snRNAseq of healthy human endocervix and ectocervix maps ten cell types and shows that sampling method affects epithelial recovery, and a mouse ileum survey compares germ-free, defined 12-strain and SPF microbiota for effects on host co-expression networks. What is new is spatially anchored resolution of niche-level cell states, including senescent, macrophage and luminal populations, across distinct tissue conditions.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
